{"author":{"id":"7674679dac9170669f752d932f8b5955b1d55717359e13b1c1e687003c7bb99f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789867519,"nonce":"oei9dkTSCGsOpFXpTC6A9lUWJGI=","message":"Decided 2026-09-19: (b), the CRDT entity — namespace `work`, so `refs/work/\u003cid\u003e`, with the schema as the singleton entity of kind `schema`.\n\nNeither stated option survived contact. (a) the worktree file is branch-coupled — an old branch silently reinterprets your issues, a bare clone has no schema — and worse, the schema has an automated writer: Jira is canonical, so sync wants to update the schema, and an automated writer has no sane way to commit to someone's worktree branch.\n\nWhat tipped it to (b) over a plain blob in a ref: the flow catalogue (b511c63) uses the same machinery — flows are basically aliases — so the op types are amortized across both, and per-key ops mean two people adding two different flows (or two different statuses) both survive, where a blob loses one silently.\n\nThe 'config ops are awkward to merge' worry is handled by making ops per-key and making order an attribute (explicit ordinal/rank) rather than a list position, so concurrent inserts don't fight over an index. Same-key conflicts fall to dag's existing lamport+op-id ordering, which is deterministic across clones.\n\nThe 'Jira sync makes the op log noisy' worry was wrong and is withdrawn: a sync that writes only on a real diff emits one op per actual Jira config change, which is the audit trail, not noise. Residue: normalize before comparing (sort by id, ignore unmapped fields) so unstable API ordering doesn't produce phantom diffs — an implementation rule for 69b7be0.\n\nReview still happens on a file: schema export/import, where import diffs and emits the minimal ops via the same reconcile(desired, current) the sync uses.\n\nFull reasoning in doc/design/configurable-schema.md, D1.","files":null}]}